cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this note, its certified optima or its code, please cite it as below. Text and data: CC BY 4.0. Code: MIT."
title: "Certified optimisation of the minimum-interaction qubit peel of \"Wave packets from the spectrum\""
authors:
  - name: "Moki & Julio"
version: "1.0"
date-released: "2026-10-11"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Hashirama1337s/certified-qubit-peel"
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - quantum mereology
  - tensor product structure
  - minimal interaction
  - qubit factorization
  - perfect matching
  - certified optimization
  - Edmonds matching polytope
  - reproducibility
abstract: >-
  Cao, Friedrich, Girard, Loizeau and Singh (arXiv:2606.10004) factorise a Hamiltonian into qubits by iterated
  minimum-interaction splits, selecting at each step the split with the smallest Tr(H_int^2)/Tr(H_q^2) from a grid of
  qubit energies optimised by BFGS. With the qubit frozen, the optimum of their sorted-level loss is a minimum-weight
  perfect matching of the levels. We solve this exactly with an LP-dual certificate, validated against an exact blossom
  solver and an independent implementation. Running the authors' published notebook unmodified on the matrix of their
  figures, the first split has ratio 0.012477 against the certified optimum 0.0062026 (+101%), on a different Bessel
  peak; over all ten splits the certified optimum beats their split by 101% to 237% at the four largest sizes and
  coincides with it from d = 128 down. The certified optima lie within 1% of E_q = 2 pi/x_i (zeros of J1), the first-pole
  condition of their Eq. (43); their printed Eq. (47) states a gap twice as large. Their leftover-interaction figure
  survives (about 2-3% at d = 2048).
